{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":224430,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2022,4,4]],"date-time":"2022-04-04T11:39:36Z","timestamp":1649072376974},"reference-count":9,"publisher":"Cambridge University Press (CUP)","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,18]],"date-time":"2020-06-18T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1592438400000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":9076,"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/terms"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Proc. annu. meet. Electron Microsc. Soc. Am."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[1995,8,13]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Diffracted beam interferometry, DBI, (previously referred to as Convergent Beam Electron Diffraction + Electron Biprism Interferometry, CBED+EBI) which uses an electron biprism to deflect diffracted beams (convergent or parallel) can produce an interferogram between any two beams within the information envelope of the microscope such that the beam's amplitude and phase can be measured and studied. As well, the electron source need not be highly coherent. So far, DBI has been applied only to transmission electron diffraction, although there is no reason why it shouldn't be applicable to all electron diffraction methods including reflection high (low) energy electron diffraction, RH(L)EED and, possibly, back-scattered electron diffraction, BSED, in the SEM for the study of surfaces. DBI has already shown that substantial phase information, such as strain at interfaces and dislocations, compositional gradients and small defect clusters which are on the size scale of the unit cell, are retrievable from its holograms.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1017\/s0424820100136957","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,18]],"date-time":"2020-06-18T09:51:31Z","timestamp":1592473891000},"page":"116-117","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Reflection diffracted beam interferometry (RDBI) applied to the study of surfaces"],"prefix":"10.1017","volume":"53","author":[{"given":"Rodney A.","family":"Herring","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"56","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,18]]},"reference":[{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib01","author":"Herring","journal-title":"these proceedings."},{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib09","first-page":"953","volume-title":"ICEM 13","volume":"VI","author":"Ma","year":"1994)"},{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib08","first-page":"828","author":"Gajdardziska-Josifovska","year":"1993","journal-title":"51st MSA meeting"},{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib03","author":"Herring","journal-title":"Ultramicroscopy"},{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib02","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/0304-3991(93)90094-E"},{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib04","first-page":"325","volume-title":"ICEM","author":"Herring","year":"1994"},{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib05","first-page":"1086","author":"Herring","year":"1993","journal-title":"51st MSA meeting"},{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib06","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1557\/PROC-332-231"},{"key":"S0424820100136957_bib07","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevLett.62.2969"}],"container-title":["Proceedings, annual meeting, Electron Microscopy Society of America"],"language":"en","link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/services\/aop-cambridge-core\/content\/view\/S0424820100136957","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,18]],"date-time":"2020-06-18T09:54:55Z","timestamp":1592474095000},"score":114.06998,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/product\/identifier\/S0424820100136957\/type\/journal_article"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[1995,8,13]]},"references-count":9,"alternative-id":["S0424820100136957"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/s0424820100136957","ISSN":["0424-8201","2690-1315"],"issn-type":[{"value":"0424-8201","type":"print"},{"value":"2690-1315","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[1995,8,13]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2022,4,2]],"date-time":"2022-04-02T03:24:23Z","timestamp":1648869863480},"reference-count":3,"publisher":"Cambridge University Press (CUP)","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,18]],"date-time":"2020-06-18T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1592438400000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":9076,"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/terms"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Proc. annu. meet. Electron Microsc. Soc. Am."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[1995,8,13]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Diffracted beam interferometry, DBI, is a practical method of interferometry which removes the strict requirement for a bright, coherent source and extends the availability of holography to a wider community of electron microscopists. The only alteration to the microscope is the installation of an electron biprism into a post-objective lens position. DBI is very flexible as the interferogram can be produced from the conditions of the infocus diffraction (Fraunhofer plane), through the Fresnel (de)focus region to the infocus specimen plane by varying the strength of the post-objective lenses, Fig. 1. As well the convergence of the interfering beams can vary from highly convergent (Fig. 1) to pseudoparallel (Fig. 2). Although the phase in the interferogram due to beam tilt and the contrast transfer function of the prefield objective lens cancel out when the interfering beams are exactly overlaid, the phase due to the spatial frequency of the biprism, <jats:bold><jats:italic>K<jats:sub>B<\/jats:sub><\/jats:italic><\/jats:bold>, the contrast transfer function, <jats:italic>u<jats:sub>L,R<\/jats:sub><\/jats:italic>, of the postfield objective lens for each beam, <jats:italic>L,R<\/jats:italic>, and the inherent material phase, <jats:italic>F<jats:sub>L,R<\/jats:sub><\/jats:italic>, remain.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1017\/s0424820100139470","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,18]],"date-time":"2020-06-18T09:51:31Z","timestamp":1592473891000},"page":"620-621","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Diffracted beam interferometry for EM and materials research"],"prefix":"10.1017","volume":"53","author":[{"given":"Rodney A.","family":"Herring","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"56","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,18]]},"reference":[{"key":"S0424820100139470_bib03","first-page":"321","volume-title":"ICEM 13","author":"Herring","year":"1994"},{"key":"S0424820100139470_bib01","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/0304-3991(93)90094-E"},{"key":"S0424820100139470_bib02","unstructured":"2. Herring, R.A. , Pozzi, G. , Tanji, T. , and Tonomura, A. , Ultramicroscopy, in press."}],"container-title":["Proceedings, annual meeting, Electron Microscopy Society of America"],"language":"en","link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/services\/aop-cambridge-core\/content\/view\/S0424820100139470","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,18]],"date-time":"2020-06-18T10:09:45Z","timestamp":1592474985000},"score":78.363884,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/product\/identifier\/S0424820100139470\/type\/journal_article"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[1995,8,13]]},"references-count":3,"alternative-id":["S0424820100139470"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/s0424820100139470","ISSN":["0424-8201","2690-1315"],"issn-type":[{"value":"0424-8201","type":"print"},{"value":"2690-1315","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[1995,8,13]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,5]],"date-time":"2025-10-05T04:32:43Z","timestamp":1759638763095},"reference-count":18,"publisher":"Oxford Univer